  "generated_text": "1. **Calculate the circumference of the sector:**\n   The circumference of the original circle is $2\\pi \\times 6 = 12\\pi$ cm. Since the circle is cut into three congruent sectors, each sector has a circumference of $\\frac{12\\pi}{3} = 4\\pi$ cm.\n\n2. **Determine the radius of the cone:**\n   When the sector is rolled into a cone, the radius of the sector becomes the radius of the base of the cone. Thus, the radius of the base of the cone is $2$ cm.\n\n3. **Use the Pythagorean theorem to find the height of the cone:**\n   The slant height of the cone is the radius of the original circle, which is $6$ cm. Using the Pythagorean theorem, the height $h$ of the cone can be calculated as follows:\n   \\[\n   h^2 = 6^2 - 2^2 = 36 - 4 = 32\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   h = \\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, $\\boxed{4\\sqrt{2}}$ cm",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[4*sqrt(2), '4\\\\sqrt{2}']",
    "gold_answer": "The circumference of the whole circle is $2 \\pi \\cdot 6 = 12 \\pi$, so the circumference of the base of the cone is $12 \\pi/3 = 4 \\pi$.  Hence, the radius of the base of the cone is $4 \\pi/(2 \\pi) = 2$.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(2 cm);\n\nfill((0,0)--arc((0,0),1,-60,60)--cycle,gray(0.7));\ndraw(Circle((0,0),1));\ndraw((0,0)--dir(60));\ndraw((0,0)--dir(180));\ndraw((0,0)--dir(300));\nlabel(\"$6$\", dir(60)/2, NW);\n[/asy]\n\nThe slant height of the cone is 6 (the radius of the original circle), so by Pythagoras, the height of the cone is $\\sqrt{6^2 - 2^2} = \\sqrt{32} = \\boxed{4 \\sqrt{2}}$.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.8 cm);\n\ndraw((-2,0)--(2,0)--(0,4*sqrt(2))--cycle);\ndraw((0,0)--(0,4*sqrt(2)));\n\nlabel(\"$2$\", (1,0), S);\nlabel(\"$6$\", (1,2*sqrt(2)), NE);\nlabel(\"$4 \\sqrt{2}$\", (0,0.7*2*sqrt(2)), W);\n[/asy]",
